---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { WARDEN_ACTION } from '../utils/version.js';
---

<Base title="GitHub Org Setup" description="Org-wide GitHub setup for Warden">
  <h1>GitHub Organization Setup</h1>

  <p>Use this when you want one org-level setup that covers many repositories. The pattern is: set credentials once at the organization level, then run Warden from an org-wide Warden root in your shared <code>.github</code> repository.</p>

  <h2>1. Set Organization Secrets</h2>

  <p>Go to <strong>Organization Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions</strong>, then add:</p>

  <dl>
    <dt>WARDEN_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</dt>
    <dd>Required. API key used by the action runtime.</dd>
    <dt>WARDEN_MODEL <em>(optional)</em></dt>
    <dd>Model override for all repos using the shared workflow.</dd>
    <dt>WARDEN_SENTRY_DSN <em>(optional)</em></dt>
    <dd>Telemetry DSN. When unset, telemetry is a no-op.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>2. Create and Install a GitHub App (Optional, Recommended)</h2>

  <p>For branded comments and org-wide repository access:</p>

  <Terminal showCopy={true}>
    <Code
      code={`npx @sentry/warden setup-app --org your-org`}
      lang="bash"
      theme="vitesse-black"
    />
  </Terminal>

  <p>The generated app uses these permissions:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>contents: write</code></li>
    <li><code>pull_requests: write</code></li>
    <li><code>issues: write</code></li>
    <li><code>checks: write</code></li>
    <li><code>metadata: read</code></li>
  </ul>

  <p>Add these org secrets after app creation:</p>
  <dl>
    <dt>WARDEN_APP_ID</dt>
    <dd>GitHub App ID.</dd>
    <dt>WARDEN_PRIVATE_KEY</dt>
    <dd>Full PEM private key contents.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>3. Define the Org Warden Root</h2>

  <p>Keep the required workflow, default config, and shared skills together in your org <code>.github</code> repository.</p>

  <Terminal showCopy={true}>
    <Code
      code={`.github/
|-- .github/workflows/warden.yml
|-- warden.toml
+-- .agents/skills/
    +-- security-review/
        |-- SKILL.md
        |-- references/
        +-- scripts/`}
      lang="text"
      theme="vitesse-black"
    />
  </Terminal>

  <p>The workflow checks out both the target repository and the org Warden root, then runs Warden with the org config.</p>

  <Terminal showCopy={true}>
    <Code
      code={`name: Warden

on:
  pull_request:
  merge_group:

jobs:
  warden:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      contents: read
    env:
      WARDEN_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY: \${{ secrets.WARDEN_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY }}
      WARDEN_MODEL: \${{ secrets.WARDEN_MODEL }}
      WARDEN_SENTRY_DSN: \${{ secrets.WARDEN_SENTRY_DSN }}
    steps:
      - uses: actions/create-github-app-token@v2
        id: app-token
        with:
          app-id: \${{ secrets.WARDEN_APP_ID }}
          private-key: \${{ secrets.WARDEN_PRIVATE_KEY }}
          owner: \${{ github.repository_owner }}

      - name: Checkout target repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v4
        with:
          ref: \${{ github.event.pull_request.head.sha || github.sha }}
          token: \${{ steps.app-token.outputs.token }}

      - name: Checkout org Warden root
        uses: actions/checkout@v4
        with:
          repository: \${{ github.repository_owner }}/.github
          path: .warden-org
          token: \${{ steps.app-token.outputs.token }}

      - uses: ${WARDEN_ACTION}
        with:
          github-token: \${{ steps.app-token.outputs.token }}
          config-path: .warden-org/warden.toml`}
      lang="yaml"
      theme="vitesse-black"
    />
  </Terminal>

  <h2>4. Roll Out Safely Across Repositories</h2>

  <p>Go to <strong>Organization Settings → Repository → Rulesets</strong>, then add an org-level ruleset:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Enforcement status: <strong>Evaluate</strong>. Failures won't block merges.</li>
    <li>Target repositories: the repos you want covered.</li>
    <li>Target branches: default branch.</li>
    <li>Require workflows to pass: select the workflow from step 3.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>This triggers Warden on every targeted repo without maintaining a workflow copy or Warden config in each one.</p>


  <p>Not every repository needs local Warden files on day one.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The org run uses <code>.warden-org/warden.toml</code> from the shared <code>.github</code> repository.</li>
    <li>Skills referenced by bare name resolve from the same org Warden root.</li>
    <li>If a repository wants its own Warden run in addition, it can add its own repo-local workflow.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. Failure Behavior</h2>

  <p>Org-wide workflows still fail for real errors:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Missing authentication or GitHub token.</li>
    <li>Missing or invalid org <code>warden.toml</code> (parse/validation errors).</li>
    <li>Action runtime failures.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
